    Virtual Desktop: give back a presenter a pass-through stopped, past the split-loop latch (V442)
    
    IL-2 Great Battles on Virtual Desktop dropped from 111 to 94 fps, with game
    latency from 5 to 12 ms, the moment CheekyFoveatedDLSS's F8 menu opened, and
    stayed there after it closed (issue 24). The menu is a cylinder layer on a
    colour swapchain of its own; creating it mid-session schedules the
    one-second swapchain_created quarantine, the pass-through frames that follow
    stop the presenter, and frame_loop_takes_presenter never asked for it again.
    IL-2 hands its whole frame loop from its loading thread to its render thread
    once, 24 s in, which latches split_frame_loop after the presenter has
    started. The latch never stopped a running presenter - without the menu the
    same session held it for the whole flight - but it refused to bring one back.
    
    A pass-through that stops a presenter now marks it to be given back
    (SessionState::presenter_restore_after_bypass), and frame_loop_takes_presenter
    lets that mark past the latch. It is set only where a running, non-pipelined
    presenter is stopped by a pass-through frame and the tray is not pausing (the
    pause keeps its own restore); never for a manual disarm or for the demotion
    after a run of generation failures. Any presenter start clears it, and so do
    xrBeginSession and xrEndSession.
    
    What does not change: the latch itself and when it is set; the mode every
    session starts in; presenter_forbidden and the DCS D3D11 workaround (the
    call site still refuses first); every runtime but Virtual Desktop, since
    frame_loop_takes_presenter returns before reading the mark there; a Virtual
    Desktop session without the latch, which was already given its presenter
    back this way.
    
    Flight log: 601 b is 1 when it gives back a presenter; new 602 (presenter
    started), 603 (stopped by a pass-through: a the reason, b marked to be given
    back) and 604 (demoted after failures). 600-604 are now kept as session
    records, so they survive a log opened late or a lost stretch - the issue-24
    log lost 115-201 s and with it every record of the stop.
    
    Test: frame-loop virtual-desktop handoff. A single-threaded session takes the
    presenter, a pass-through stops it, the loop moves to another thread; the
    presenter has to come back, and 603/600/601/602 have to say so. On this fake
    runtime the stop comes from the quarantine its first presenter pair causes,
    not from an overlay's swapchain; the latch and the give-back are the same.
    It fails without the change (presenter never back).
    
    ctest 88/88; the presenter, DCS, frame-loop and pause scenarios looped eight
    times each. Not yet run in a headset.
    
    Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>

